repository-code: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/reconstruction-independent-moment-tests"
url: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/reconstruction-independent-moment-tests"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23374014"
version: "2.0.3"
date-released: "2026-11-17"
abstract: >-
  Code and data for "What low-order sum rules can and cannot detect in sample-based spectral functions"
  (earlier title: "Reconstruction-independent moment tests for sample-based spectral functions"). Full-operator
  moments <J†(H-e0)^k J> of the probe J, evaluated on the subspace ground state of energy e0, differ from the
  reconstruction's moments exactly by the leak of the probe state out of the subspace, order by order, so a
  pipeline can test the closure of its own output; the textbook double-commutator form lacks this property and,
  on a truncated Hubbard-ring subspace, passes a reconstruction whose first moment is 11% low. With exact
  moments plus shot noise, the m1 and m2 checks detect weight moved in frequency at fixed total weight. In a
  sealed blinded simulation (300 instances) on a doped Hubbard ring, the sealed primary endpoint was a
  detection rate of 101/188 at one false positive in 112 (40/112 at the 1.96-fold noise scale the sealed text
  specified); the battery rejected all 56 sampled truncations and none of 71 under-converged Krylov
  reconstructions. Through m2 no check has guaranteed power against weight moved among four or more fixed
  poles; by the classical Gauss-quadrature property of the Lanczos recursion an n-node reconstruction is
  invisible below order 2n, and its first residual, relative to m_2n, is at most 4[D/(4w)]^(2n) for a probe
  spectrum of width D and mean excitation energy w. On IBM Heron (ibm_fez) samples, with all moments computed
  classically, captured weight rewards support spreading, not fidelity. The repository holds the exact
  sector-Lanczos/Haydock engine, the generators behind the quoted numbers and figures, the sealed records, the
  raw counts and reference samplers of the device run, and the record of the sealed on-device n_k test (not
  run; no job submitted).
